:::


::: callout-tip
## Why `list(Sales)` and not just `Sales`

When I write `summarise(\`Monthly Sales\` = list(Sales))`, each product collapses to one row whose cell holds all twelve monthly values bundled as a list. That is what lets `gt_plt_sparkline()` draw a whole mini chart inside one cell. If I forget the `list()` and pass a plain vector, the function has nothing to plot per row and the call fails. This list-column requirement is the thing I most need to remember about `gtExtras`.
:::

Now the sparkline table.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
report %>%
  group_by(Product) %>%
  summarise(`Monthly Sales` = list(Sales), .groups = "drop") %>%
  gt() %>%
  gt_plt_sparkline(`Monthly Sales`, same_limit = FALSE)
```

